Subject: [PATCH 0016] smoothSolver: form GaussSeidel residual from the final
 sweep

F3 rounding change; not bit-identical. No solver iteration is elided and no
choice depends on the benchmark's iteration limits. Apply to smoothSolver
with the GaussSeidel smoother, positive nSweeps and nonBlocking comms, on
symmetric or asymmetric matrices with ordinary processor interfaces (or no
interfaces). Both scalar solve() and solveGaussSeidelComponents() use the
same templated sweep. Other smoothers, non-positive nSweeps and unsupported
interfaces/comms retain the original sweep plus explicit residual path.

Let x be the solution before the last forward sweep, y after it, and B(x)
the RHS interface sum produced by init/updateMatrixInterfaces(false).
OpenFOAM processor fields use !add when accumulating their coefficients,
so this flag gives +coeff*receivedPsi; the complete operator is
A = D + L + U - B. With lowerAddr[f] < upperAddr[f], upper[f] belongs to
the owner row and lower[f] to the neighbour row. The sweep computes

  n_i = b_i + B(x)_i - sum(j<i) L_ij*y_j - sum(j>i) U_ij*x_j
  y_i = n_i/D_i
  r_i = (n_i - D_i*y_i) - sum(j>i) U_ij*(y_j-x_j) + B(y)_i-B(x)_i.

The last expression is b-A*y in exact arithmetic. Retain the local
division defect n_i-D_i*y_i, rather than assuming the division is exact.
Row-formation roundoff and the residual additions are reassociated, so
very small residuals can differ at the floating-point noise floor. This
is a fresh residual construction every iteration, not a recurrence over
previous residuals. Compiler FMA contraction settings are unchanged.

Keep the existing source/interface/lower accumulator operations, upper
subtractions, division and lower scatter for psi. When y_i is available,
use losortStart/losortAddr to visit incoming faces and scatter
-upper[f]*(y_i-x_i) into already-initialised owner residuals. Every target
has an index below i and will not be reset later. The owner-row layout
cannot scatter these corrections in the existing outgoing-face loop:
there is still a U-only incoming traversal. It replaces the separate
D/b cell pass and U/L residual face pass, not all residual memory traffic.
For one sweep, coefficient streams fall from 4*faces to 3*faces; the extra
losort traversal and its addressing must be included in performance tests.
Move the existing component sweep into a private header so both scalar
and batched paths share this kernel and the standalone test can exercise
exactly the production implementation.

Keep B(psi) in a solve-local field, and keep old boundary-cell sums in a
compact array during exchange. Only distinct boundary cells are reset,
seeded and completed: repeated faces or patches must not duplicate a sum.
After the final sweep, exchange psi_new, add Bnew-Bold to the residual,
and leave Bnew ready to seed the next sweep. Thus N iterations of s sweeps
use N*s+1 interface exchanges per component instead of N*(s+1), excluding
the unchanged initial Amul. Earlier sweeps of an iteration remain ordinary
GS sweeps. The first iteration still has its startup exchange. State is
never reused across solves or component fields.

Support is checked for every component, with one communicator-wide AND
before using the new exchange schedule. A local-only fallback could
mismatch neighbours' exchanges. Restrict interface types to the ordinary
"processor" implementation, whose updates are additive and touch only
patch-addressed cells; cyclic, AMI, overset, custom interfaces and other
communication modes retain the original path. The scalar path avoids
constructing an unused GaussSeidelSmoother/accumulator when fused.

Initial Amul, normalisation, initial residual, setResidualField hooks,
gSumMag communicator reductions, convergence tests, nSweeps increments,
minIter/maxIter handling, and per-component active states remain in their
existing positions. The final field hook receives the complete new
residual. Components can converge separately and switch from the N=3 to
N=1 kernel. Tolerance-controlled solves do the same numerical work, up
to rounding in the residual, as fixed-limit solves.

Validation: GCC 13.3 -fsyntax-only on smoothSolver.C (all sweep template
instantiations) and simpleFoam.C (fvMatrix/vector call site). Standalone
random diagonally dominant systems: 6400 cases per FMA setting, scalar
and three components, one/two sweeps, symmetric/asymmetric coefficients,
uncoupled/fake two-rank interfaces, repeated boundary cells, empty face
rows, separate component convergence, absolute/relative tolerances and
minIter. Both -ffp-contract=off and fast give identical psi iterates and
identical stopping counts; maximum normalised residual-norm differences
are 1.83e-15 and 1.64e-15. Full OpenFOAM serial and 1/2/4-node Cd checks
remain external acceptance tests; the required bound is +/-0.5 percent.
